{"title":"1995 USAMO Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose that in a certain society, each pair of persons can be classified as either <i>amicable</i> or <i>hostile</i>. We shall say that each member of an amicable pair is a <i>friend</i> of the other, and each member of a hostile pair is a  <i>foe</i> of the other.  Suppose that the society has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/f/f3fe67cd85ba57a3c30adc14b0b68a259e69d403.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\, n \\,$\" width=\"13\" height=\"8\" /> persons and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/2/252bd9794edd4df96b45c8d9d775440a737db9c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\, q \\,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"11\" height=\"11\" /> amicable pairs, and that for every set of three persons, at least one pair is hostile.  Prove that there is at least one member of the society whose foes include <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/3/123271d6b63afbce30018715324abf325d4671aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\, q(1 - 4q/n^2) \\,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"19\" /> or fewer amicable pairs.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider the graph with two people joined if they are friends.\n</p><p>For each person <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/8/c18525311fb5d25a98f9cfa481d89cf368deaf51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> be the set of its friends and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/c/fec9b936fc218898fb8ff298c449d24f3fde3985.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" /> the set of its foes. Note that any edge goes either: from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/8/c18525311fb5d25a98f9cfa481d89cf368deaf51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> (type 1), from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/8/c18525311fb5d25a98f9cfa481d89cf368deaf51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/c/fec9b936fc218898fb8ff298c449d24f3fde3985.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" /> (type 2) or from a point of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/c/fec9b936fc218898fb8ff298c449d24f3fde3985.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" /> to another (type 3), but there's no edge joining two points of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/8/c18525311fb5d25a98f9cfa481d89cf368deaf51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> (since they would form a triangle with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />). Let the number of type 1, type 2, type 3 edges of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/1/3f10c443fbea66f267d37de0c0e8a7014c01301c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1, x_2, x_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"68\" height=\"11\" /> respectively, so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/a/ccada11db7b2b90693e2fac4f887a57fce6f96bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> is the degree of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and we want to show that for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/1/2b12a6c2704fdf04a90eae2c5e4bfa1a14d6cf21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_3 \\leq q(1-4q/n^2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"140\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p><p>Since each edge is of one of those types, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/0/bf0c51998b054d4447a0ffd60c10328a2322992d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1+x_2+x_3=q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"130\" height=\"14\" />. Thus\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/c/abc329d6a5378e1308afd9543f7fd19646b75469.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x_3 \\leq q(1-4q/n^2) \\Longleftrightarrow \\\\ x_3 \\leq q-4q^2/n^2 \\Longleftrightarrow \\\\ q-x_1-x_2 \\leq q-4q^2/n^2 \\Longleftrightarrow \\\\ x_1+x_2 \\geq 4q^2/n^2.\\]\" width=\"684\" height=\"20\" />\nThat is, what we want is equivalent to proving that for some vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, the set of edges touching either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> or a vertex joined to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/2/9f226cd2cc7a8a79da7ca38e252e18558d450eb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4q^2/n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"19\" />. Obviously now we'll sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/c/87cf8bc498151d722a3b34591ca7468d43e95939.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1+x_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" /> over all vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. In the resulting sum, an edge joining <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/8/788f3c8df73c43990991c6f116e759e1e1116b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X, Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" /> is counted once for each edge that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/8/788f3c8df73c43990991c6f116e759e1e1116b47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X, Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" /> have, that is, it is counted <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/e/17ec129adb982d55bf8cdf7138414499ce2876a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(X)+D(Y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" /> times, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/9/579dcd5941507c6f5ca59df256078e40769c2305.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(X)=x_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"18\" /> is the degree of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. Thus each vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> contributes to the overall sum with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/c/26cd28f965829760d8c258d11c15d14a67ccb9bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"18\" /> for each edge it has, and since it has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/c/26cd28f965829760d8c258d11c15d14a67ccb9bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"18\" /> edges, it contributes with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/2/d125c8b534eea4c54ecaa63c5f1893792f897d97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(X)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"19\" />. Thus the considered sum is equal to\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/7/0574b4105595f6b9868d4858fbce79b936f12a9f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{X \\in G} D(X)^2 \\geq \\frac{(\\sum_{X \\in G} D(X))^2}{n}=\\frac{(2q)^2}{n}=4q^2/n.\\]\" width=\"377\" height=\"48\" />\n(That's Cauchy.) Since we are summing over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> vertices, one of the summands is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/2/9f226cd2cc7a8a79da7ca38e252e18558d450eb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4q^2/n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"19\" /> by pigeonhole, which is what we wanted to prove.\n</p><p><i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO\" title=\"1995 USAMO\">1995 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1995 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1995\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1995 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Problem</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1995 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1995 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1995 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1995_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1995 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017185445\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.043 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.066 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 241/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1586/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 50/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6995/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   21.005      1 -total\n 38.03%    7.989      1 Template:USAMO_box\n 30.99%    6.510      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n 28.95%    6.081      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:15088:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017185445 and revision id 202843. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}